WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.170 --> 00:00:01.980
- If you've taken a nap at any point

00:00:01.980 --> 00:00:03.340
in the last couple of months,

00:00:03.340 --> 00:00:04.480
there's a good chance you've missed

00:00:04.480 --> 00:00:06.980
at least one US threat to impose tariffs

00:00:06.980 --> 00:00:10.340
on hundreds of billions
of dollars of US imports.

00:00:10.340 --> 00:00:11.670
- Tariffs are a a beautiful thing.

00:00:11.670 --> 00:00:12.700
It's a beautiful word.

00:00:12.700 --> 00:00:13.830
We're gonna put tariffs on them.

00:00:13.830 --> 00:00:16.160
Those tariffs were ready
to go on Monday morning.

00:00:16.160 --> 00:00:17.930
We always have the option to raise it

00:00:17.930 --> 00:00:19.320
another $300 billion.

00:00:19.320 --> 00:00:20.790
- As part of the President's efforts

00:00:20.790 --> 00:00:22.640
to rewrite the global rules of trade

00:00:22.640 --> 00:00:24.670
and rebalance our trading relationships,

00:00:24.670 --> 00:00:27.440
he's imposed tariffs on almost
every country in the world

00:00:27.440 --> 00:00:29.100
over the past year and a half.

00:00:29.100 --> 00:00:31.300
That includes our
biggest trading partners,

00:00:31.300 --> 00:00:33.780
China, Canada, Mexico, and Europe.

00:00:33.780 --> 00:00:35.690
Recently, a lot has been happening.

00:00:35.690 --> 00:00:37.220
We're going to try to
bring you up to speed.

00:00:37.220 --> 00:00:39.970
So let's start with our
biggest trading partner, China.

00:00:40.870 --> 00:00:43.940
The US imported about $540 billion

00:00:43.940 --> 00:00:45.600
of goods from China last year

00:00:45.600 --> 00:00:48.110
and exported about $120 billion,

00:00:48.110 --> 00:00:50.140
making it the biggest trading relationship

00:00:50.140 --> 00:00:51.880
of any two countries in the world.

00:00:51.880 --> 00:00:53.630
One of President Trump's big goals

00:00:53.630 --> 00:00:55.710
is to balance this relationship out.

00:00:55.710 --> 00:00:58.210
Last summer, he slapped
25% tariffs on goods

00:00:58.210 --> 00:01:01.560
like machinery, commodities,
semi-conductors, and plastics.

00:01:01.560 --> 00:01:02.930
China retaliated,

00:01:02.930 --> 00:01:05.470
mostly on vehicles and
agricultural products,

00:01:05.470 --> 00:01:06.870
especially soy-beans.

00:01:06.870 --> 00:01:08.400
The US hit back in the fall.

00:01:08.400 --> 00:01:11.920
This time, with 10% tariffs
on an additional $200 billion

00:01:11.920 --> 00:01:15.340
worth of products,
furniture, handbags, luggage,

00:01:15.340 --> 00:01:16.940
and many computer and auto parts.

00:01:16.940 --> 00:01:18.980
China once again retaliated.

00:01:18.980 --> 00:01:21.630
In December, the US and
China called a truce

00:01:21.630 --> 00:01:23.020
but it didn't last.

00:01:23.020 --> 00:01:25.660
The talks broke down in May
and the US moved forward

00:01:25.660 --> 00:01:29.220
with plans to raise
those 10% tariffs to 25%.

00:01:29.220 --> 00:01:31.770
China retaliated and
then the US threatened

00:01:31.770 --> 00:01:33.850
to impose tariffs on everything else

00:01:33.850 --> 00:01:35.300
that comes in from China,

00:01:35.300 --> 00:01:38.580
an additional $300 billion of goods.

00:01:38.580 --> 00:01:40.210
So where do things stand now?

00:01:40.210 --> 00:01:42.470
President Trump and China's
President, Xi Jinping,

00:01:42.470 --> 00:01:44.050
held a summit on June 29th

00:01:44.050 --> 00:01:45.606
where they reached a truce for now.

00:01:45.606 --> 00:01:48.780
- We're going to work with China

00:01:48.780 --> 00:01:53.430
on where we left off to
see if we can make a deal.

00:01:53.430 --> 00:01:56.380
- The US will hold off on
its final round of tariffs

00:01:56.380 --> 00:01:59.180
but all the original rounds
of tariffs will still remain

00:01:59.180 --> 00:02:00.480
and if they can't get a deal,

00:02:00.480 --> 00:02:03.590
it's likely we'll be talking
about tariffs again soon.

00:02:03.590 --> 00:02:06.580
But the trade tensions
haven't just been with China.

00:02:06.580 --> 00:02:09.460
Canada and Mexico have also
been in the US crosshairs.

00:02:09.460 --> 00:02:12.140
- I have long contended that NAFTA

00:02:12.140 --> 00:02:14.990
was perhaps the worst
trade deal ever made.

00:02:14.990 --> 00:02:17.940
- About $600 billion a year worth of trade

00:02:17.940 --> 00:02:22.570
flows between Canada and between Mexico

00:02:22.570 --> 00:02:24.560
and the United States each year.

00:02:24.560 --> 00:02:26.590
One of President Trump's
big campaign goals

00:02:26.590 --> 00:02:28.160
was to rewrite the 25-year-old

00:02:28.160 --> 00:02:30.770
North American Free Trade
Agreement, or NAFTA.

00:02:30.770 --> 00:02:33.740
The talks started in 2017 but last year,

00:02:33.740 --> 00:02:35.980
when they weren't making
progress quickly enough,

00:02:35.980 --> 00:02:37.390
President Trump imposed tariffs

00:02:37.390 --> 00:02:38.830
on steel and aluminum imports

00:02:38.830 --> 00:02:40.743
from everyone in the the world,

00:02:40.743 --> 00:02:41.590
including Mexico and Canada

00:02:41.590 --> 00:02:44.040
and to further up the
pressure he threatened tariffs

00:02:44.040 --> 00:02:46.570
on the entire global automobile industry,

00:02:46.570 --> 00:02:49.890
about $372 billion worth of imports.

00:02:49.890 --> 00:02:52.860
Last summer, the three countries
finally reached a new deal.

00:02:52.860 --> 00:02:55.080
The US-Mexico-Canada agreement.

00:02:55.080 --> 00:02:57.080
- Called USMCA, sort of just works, MCA.

00:03:00.000 --> 00:03:01.730
- But the deal doesn't go into effect

00:03:01.730 --> 00:03:03.970
until all three countries
have ratified it.

00:03:03.970 --> 00:03:06.480
So far, only Mexico has done so.

00:03:06.480 --> 00:03:07.960
To ease the road to getting it passed,

00:03:07.960 --> 00:03:09.170
the US did two things.

00:03:09.170 --> 00:03:11.430
First, it dropped the
threat of auto tariffs

00:03:11.430 --> 00:03:13.000
against Canada and Mexico

00:03:13.000 --> 00:03:15.250
and second, in May, it finally removed

00:03:15.250 --> 00:03:17.010
the tariffs against steel and aluminum

00:03:17.010 --> 00:03:19.980
and Mexico and Canada
removed their tariffs too

00:03:19.980 --> 00:03:21.730
but then, things got complicated.

00:03:21.730 --> 00:03:24.470
- Mexico shouldn't
allow millions of people

00:03:24.470 --> 00:03:25.950
to try and enter their country

00:03:25.950 --> 00:03:28.350
and they can stop it very quickly

00:03:28.350 --> 00:03:29.290
and I think they will

00:03:29.290 --> 00:03:32.050
and if they won't, we're
gonna put tariffs on them.

00:03:32.050 --> 00:03:34.520
- President Trump,
citing too many migrants

00:03:34.520 --> 00:03:36.530
are arriving at the US-Mexico border,

00:03:36.530 --> 00:03:40.270
threatened tariffs on everything
the US imports from Mexico,

00:03:40.270 --> 00:03:43.120
about $350 billion a year of goods.

00:03:43.120 --> 00:03:46.990
- Those tariffs go from
5%, to 10%, to 15%,

00:03:46.990 --> 00:03:48.770
to 20%, and then to 25%.

00:03:48.770 --> 00:03:51.460
- Mexico rushed to delegation to DC,

00:03:51.460 --> 00:03:53.770
pledged to do more at
its own souther border

00:03:53.770 --> 00:03:55.300
to stop the flow of migrants

00:03:55.300 --> 00:03:57.930
and avoided the threat of tariffs for now.

00:03:57.930 --> 00:03:59.300
But President Trump has made it clear

00:03:59.300 --> 00:04:00.360
that the threat remains

00:04:00.360 --> 00:04:02.680
and will be revisted within 90 days.

00:04:02.680 --> 00:04:04.670
But when it comes to
trading relationships,

00:04:04.670 --> 00:04:07.960
there's an even bigger one
that's still unsettled.

00:04:07.960 --> 00:04:09.820
We're talking about Europe.

00:04:09.820 --> 00:04:12.550
- If you look at the European Union,

00:04:12.550 --> 00:04:15.870
it's very solidly against
us in terms of trade.

00:04:15.870 --> 00:04:17.840
- The European Union sets trade policies

00:04:17.840 --> 00:04:19.680
for all its member countries

00:04:19.680 --> 00:04:23.890
so collectively, the US imports
about $500 billion a year

00:04:23.890 --> 00:04:25.220
of goods from the EU

00:04:25.220 --> 00:04:28.700
and exports about $320
billion making the US

00:04:28.700 --> 00:04:32.170
in new trade the biggest trading
relationship in the world.

00:04:32.170 --> 00:04:34.340
Remember those steel and aluminum tariffs?

00:04:34.340 --> 00:04:36.290
Yeah, they still apply to Europe

00:04:36.290 --> 00:04:39.070
and there's a couple big
threats still hanging out there.

00:04:39.070 --> 00:04:40.720
First, autos.

00:04:40.720 --> 00:04:41.931
- They send the Mercedes.

00:04:41.931 --> 00:04:43.610
They send BMWs.

00:04:43.610 --> 00:04:45.170
They send everything.

00:04:45.170 --> 00:04:47.170
We tax them practically nothing.

00:04:47.170 --> 00:04:48.720
We can't send our cars.

00:04:48.720 --> 00:04:51.240
- The US imports a lot
of cars from Europe.

00:04:51.240 --> 00:04:52.484
For now, the Trump administration

00:04:52.484 --> 00:04:55.320
has postponed the threat of
auto tariffs until November,

00:04:55.320 --> 00:04:56.310
but not removed it.

00:04:56.310 --> 00:04:57.950
There's also a threat of tariffs

00:04:57.950 --> 00:04:59.340
because of a long running dispute

00:04:59.340 --> 00:05:01.080
between Airbus and Boeing.

00:05:01.080 --> 00:05:02.887
The EU and US have each threatened

00:05:02.887 --> 00:05:05.570
$11 billion of tariffs because of this.

00:05:05.570 --> 00:05:08.060
Negotiators here aren't
making any progress

00:05:08.060 --> 00:05:09.676
and the Europeans are getting worried.

00:05:09.676 --> 00:05:11.660
President Trump has already shown

00:05:11.660 --> 00:05:12.950
he's willing to threaten tariffs

00:05:12.950 --> 00:05:15.470
against everything the
US imports from China

00:05:15.470 --> 00:05:18.350
and everything the US imports from Mexico.

00:05:18.350 --> 00:05:19.700
Put it all together and there's over

00:05:19.700 --> 00:05:24.120
$2.5 trillion of trade
hanging in the balance.

00:05:24.120 --> 00:05:25.670
So far, the Trump Administration

00:05:25.670 --> 00:05:29.010
hasn't gotten any major
trade deals across the line

00:05:29.010 --> 00:05:32.530
and so the worry is that they
could keep adding tariffs.

00:05:32.530 --> 00:05:35.380
A billion dollars here and
a billion dollars there

00:05:35.380 --> 00:05:37.963
and pretty soon, you're
talking about real money.

